---
// Comparison page. Copy authored against briefs/maltipoo-vs-poodle.json (2026-07-13).
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import AnswerCapsule from '../components/AnswerCapsule.astro';
import CaptionedBlock from '../components/CaptionedBlock.astro';
import FactsCard from '../components/FactsCard.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import CtaClose from '../components/CtaClose.astro';
import { site, whatsappLink } from '../data/site';
import { webPageSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../lib/schema';
import featuredImg from '../assets/site/20-small-breeds-lineup.webp';

const title = 'Maltipoo vs Toy Poodle: The Honest Trade | Maltipoo.sg';
const description =
  'Maltipoo vs Toy Poodle for Singapore flats: athletic biddable purebred vs softer velcro cross. Coat, energy, barking and HDB status compared honestly.';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Compare Breeds', url: '/compare-maltipoo' },
  { name: 'Maltipoo vs Poodle', url: '/maltipoo-vs-poodle' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Which is smarter, a Maltipoo or a Toy Poodle?',
    a: 'The Toy Poodle, by a nose; it is one of the sharpest breeds there is. The Maltipoo inherits enough of that mind to train quickly, and for a first-time owner the difference in practice is small.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is grooming the same for both?',
    a: 'Same salon price tier by weight, different forgiveness. The Poodle’s tight curl mats fast when brushing slips; the Maltipoo’s looser wave gives you a day or two of grace. In our humidity, neither coat tolerates neglect.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which barks more?',
    a: 'They are close: both are alert-barkers, and in both, loneliness is the loudest trigger. Early quiet training and daytime company matter more than the breed choice.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I get a Toy Poodle from you?',
    a: 'This shop specialises in Maltipoos; our parent brand Curious Tails handles other breeds. WhatsApp us and we route you to whichever side fits, honestly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is better for allergy-sensitive homes?',
    a: 'The Poodle, marginally, thanks to the tighter curl. But no dog is truly hypoallergenic, so meet the puppy in person before committing either way.',
  },
];

const schemas = [
  webPageSchema({
    path: '/maltipoo-vs-poodle',
    title,
    description,
    aboutName: 'Maltipoo vs Toy Poodle comparison',
    datePublished: '2026-07-13',
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbs),
  faqSchema(faqItems),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/maltipoo-vs-poodle" schemas={schemas}>
  <section class="container-site pt-4">
    <div class="rounded-[var(--radius-panel)] bg-tint-200 px-6 py-10 md:px-12">
      <Breadcrumb items={crumbs} bare class="mb-5" />
      <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Comparison</p>
      <h1 class="text-[clamp(1.9rem,4vw,2.8rem)] mb-3 max-w-[24ch]">Maltipoo vs Toy Poodle</h1>
      <p class="text-ink-soft max-w-[52ch] mb-5">
        One of Singapore's most-owned breeds against its own cross. The trade is real:
        athleticism and edge for softness and lap time.
      </p>
      <a href={whatsappLink('Hi! Could you help me decide between a Maltipoo and a Toy Poodle?')} class="btn-cta">Ask which fits you</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <AnswerCapsule question="Maltipoo or Toy Poodle: what is the difference?">
    The difference between Maltipoo and Toy Poodle is a fair trade. A Toy Poodle is the athletic scholar: tightly curled coat, the sharpest training mind in
    the small-dog class, and more independence. A Maltipoo trades some of that athleticism
    for softer, heavier-on-the-lap devotion and a looser coat. Both are HDB-approved and sit
    in the 2 to 5kg range; pick by energy and attachment, not looks.
  </AnswerCapsule>

  <section class="container-site pt-8">
    <img
      src={featuredImg.src}
      width={featuredImg.width}
      height={featuredImg.height}
      alt="Maltipoo sitting beside an apricot Toy Poodle among other small breeds"
      loading="lazy"
      class="rounded-[var(--radius-media)] w-full h-auto shadow-[var(--shadow-pop)]"
    />
  </section>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Mind and energy: scholar vs shadow" id="mind">
    <p>
      The maltipoo vs toy poodle gap is widest here. A Toy Poodle wants a job: longer walks,
      trick sessions, puzzle toys, and it repays the effort with obedience that looks like
      showing off. It also holds its own alone better than most toy breeds.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Maltipoo keeps enough Poodle mind to train fast, then spends the rest of the day
      being a shadow. Its job is you, as the
      <a href="/maltipoo-temperament">temperament guide</a> spells out, and it needs daytime
      company more than it needs a fifth walk.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Energy and mind"
      items={[
        'Toy Poodle: needs jobs, walks, puzzles',
        'Maltipoo: 15 to 20 minute walks, lap-first',
        'Both: quick learners, easy toilet training',
        'Alone tolerance: Poodle higher, honestly',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Coat: tight curl vs soft wave" id="coat" reverse>
    <p>
      Both coats are low-shedding and both arrive in apricot, which is why the breeds get
      mistaken for each other around Balestier. The Poodle's tight curl demands a strict
      brushing cadence or it mats hard; the Maltipoo's looser wave is a touch more forgiving,
      though Singapore humidity forgives neither. Same grooming price tier by weight, per the
      <a href="/maltipoo-size">size guide</a> maths.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Coat care, honestly"
      items={[
        'Poodle: tight curl, strict brushing schedule',
        'Maltipoo: soft wave, slightly forgiving',
        'Both: groom every 4 to 6 weeks here',
        'Both: apricot common, low-shedding',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Flats, barking and HDB" id="hdb" tinted>
    <p>
      No paperwork drama on either side: the Toy and Miniature Poodle is named at item 42 of
      the
      <a href="https://www.hdb.gov.sg/managing-my-home/home-ownership/keeping-pets" rel="noopener" target="_blank">HDB approved list</a>,
      and the Maltipoo qualifies via item 62. Both alert-bark at corridor sounds; in both, an
      empty flat is the real volume knob.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Flat facts"
      items={[
        'HDB: Poodle item 42, Maltipoo via item 62',
        'Size: both toy class for flats',
        'Barking: similar, loneliness-driven',
        'Licence: same AVS rules for both',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Who should pick which" id="verdict">
    <p>
      Pick the Toy Poodle if you enjoy training as a hobby, want a small dog that hikes above
      its weight, and are away moderate hours. Pick the Maltipoo if what you actually want is
      the Poodle look with a softer, clingier temperament, because that is literally what the
      cross is.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owners often tell us their maltipoo looks more like Poodle than Maltese; that is the curl talking, and it is why the toy poodle vs maltipoo confusion is so common at the park. A note from the shop floor: half the buyers who arrive asking for a Toy Poodle describe
      their ideal dog and describe a Maltipoo. Describe yours on WhatsApp
      ({site.phoneDisplay}) and we will tell you which one you just described.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="The one-line verdict"
      items={[
        'Trainer-type, active owner: Toy Poodle',
        'Company-rich lap home: Maltipoo',
        'Want poodle looks, softer heart: Maltipoo',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <FAQ items={faqItems} />

  <CtaClose
    secondaryHeading="Compare more breeds"
    secondaryText="Cavapoo, Maltese, Bichon, Shih Tzu and Cockapoo, all against the Maltipoo."
    secondaryHref="/compare-maltipoo"
    secondaryLabel="See all comparisons"
  />
</BaseLayout>
